{
  "summary": "The American Football Union (AFU) was a New York–area league of amateur, collegiate, and semi-professional football clubs that ran from 1886 to 1895. Formed by eight clubs in January 1886, it established shared rules, schedules, and a championship pennant, but early seasons were marked by clubs joining and withdrawing, disputed games, and incomplete schedules.\n\nThe Crescent Athletic Club became the league’s dominant team, winning the championship from 1887 through 1892. The Orange Athletic Club joined in 1888, upset Crescent in 1890, and later won three consecutive titles from 1893 to 1895. The article leaves the 1895 championship uncertain, listing both Orange and Crescent as possible winners.\n\nThe AFU had little influence on the development of professional football, but Orange’s later history gave the league a notable connection to the NFL: the club eventually became the Orange and Newark Tornadoes, which played in the NFL in 1929 and 1930.",
